0.04736328125, 0.037933349609375, 0.032379150390625, 0.01293182373046875, 0.007251739501953125, -0.0238494873046875, -0.0386962890625, -0.0021209716796875, 0.0069427490234375, 0.01515960693359375, -0.0190277099609375, -0.00972747802734375, 0.006374359130859375, -0.0218963623046875, -0.01493072509765625, -0.01079559326171875, -0.0001404285430908203, 0.03857421875, -0.00916290283203125, -0.01001739501953125, -0.0162200927734375, -0.0211944580078125, 0.0100555419921875, -0.0203857421875, 0.022613525390625, -0.0016374588012695312, 0.0178375244140625, -0.00847625732421875, -0.04412841796875, 0.00554656982421875, 0.002002716064453125, 0.00372314453125, -0.025238037109375, -0.00933837890625, 0.04058837890625, -0.01374053955078125, -0.0033130645751953125, -0.0244293212890625, -0.004634857177734375, 0.034027099609375, 0.001392364501953125, 0.0276947021484375, 0.01160430908203125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to investigate the use of Geometric Algebra (GA) in the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly electrical circuits. A key aspect of this process is transforming the ordinary differential equations that describe system behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain. This transformation requires a definition of the Laplace transform within GA. In this work, we build upon previous studies that extended the Laplace transform to certain hyper-complex algebras by introducing a definition of the Laplace transform specifically within the framework of Geometric Algebra. Our definition and its associated properties are applicable to geometric algebras with signature less than or equal to 5."}}